WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.750
My robot needed twelve cards to walk round

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:07.030
the playground square: forward, forward,

3
00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:11.070
forward, turn, again and again.

4
00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:14.490
Then a pupil said: Miss, it’s

5
00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.290
the same thing four times!

6
00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.540
She had just discovered loops.

7
00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.130
Today: loops, and how they

8
00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:23.330
make programs shorter.

9
00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:26.030
Look at the robot’s program.

10
00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:29.670
Forward three times, then turn right.

11
00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.570
Then the same again, and again, and again.

12
00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:36.890
When steps repeat, there is a pattern,

13
00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.470
and a pattern can become a loop.

14
00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.570
A loop repeats a block of steps a number

15
00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:46.310
of times, or until something happens.

16
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.990
Instead of twelve cards, you write: repeat

17
00:00:49.990 --> 00:00:53.450
four times, forward three, turn right.

18
00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:56.550
Four cards do the work of twelve.

19
00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.290
Loops make programs shorter, but

20
00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:01.970
that is not the best part.

21
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:03.600
Want a bigger square?

22
00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:07.250
Change one number, not twelve cards.

23
00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:10.050
Fewer cards means fewer

24
00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:11.710
places for a bug to hide.

25
00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:14.790
Children already know loops.

26
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.400
A song’s chorus comes back

27
00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.480
after every verse.

28
00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.490
A clapping game repeats the same pattern.

29
00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:24.560
A dance says: step, step,

30
00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:26.640
clap, four times.

31
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.760
Writing it once and saying how many

32
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:32.320
times is exactly what a loop does.

33
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:34.220
Quick check!

34
00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:39.030
My program is six forward cards in a row.

35
00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:43.660
If one card can repeat up to four times,

36
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.340
how few cards can do the same walk?

37
00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.670
Two cards: forward four

38
00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:52.850
times, then forward twice.

39
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.800
One card can’t do six, because a

40
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.220
card repeats at most four times.

41
00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:00.690
Limits like this make pupils

42
00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.590
think, and that’s the point.

43
00:02:03.910 --> 00:02:06.410
Later, the same idea becomes code.

44
00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.440
In Python, a loop starts with

45
00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.920
for, and the lines inside it are

46
00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:12.980
pushed in from the edge.

47
00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:15.310
This one walks the whole square.

48
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.780
The robot cards and the code

49
00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:19.000
are the same thinking.

50
00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:22.270
Before running a loop, ask

51
00:02:22.270 --> 00:02:23.370
pupils to predict.

52
00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:26.230
How many squares will the robot walk?

53
00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.650
Where will it stop, and

54
00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:29.870
which way will it face?

55
00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.560
Predicting first turns

56
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:33.900
guessing into thinking.

57
00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.770
In the app, open Program a robot

58
00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:39.830
and try grids four to six.

59
00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.840
Tap a card in your program to repeat it.

60
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.680
Then go for three stars: the

61
00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.120
fewest cards possible.

62
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:48.940
Your turn.

63
00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.350
My robot runs: repeat three

64
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.370
times, forward two.

65
00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.540
How many squares does it move?

66
00:02:57.790 --> 00:02:58.130
Six.

67
00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:01.430
Remember: repeated steps are a pattern.

68
00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:05.060
A loop repeats a block a number of times.

69
00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:07.130
It makes programs shorter

70
00:03:07.130 --> 00:03:08.550
and easier to change.

71
00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.550
And pupils already know loops

72
00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.030
from songs and games.

73
00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:16.820
Try it in class tomorrow: dance loops.

74
00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:20.840
Groups invent a short dance, write it as a

75
00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:23.780
program with repeat, and swap programs.

76
00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:26.040
Can another group perform it

77
00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.440
exactly from the paper?
